## SECTION 4, Showcase the Nominee  *(hard limit 400 words)*

> *Prompt: showcase the distinguished, sustained and conspicuous contributions at a national level, "the unique and distinguished nature of the service that can be seen as a unique contribution to the advancement of Surf Life Saving Australia."*

**Version 1, original concise draft [56 / 400 words]**

> Chris has spent twenty years advancing lifesaving nationally, 14 on the Lifesaving Members Advisory Committee and 9 as National Chair. He reshaped how every Australian lifesaver trains through the curriculum review, PSAR manual rewrite and three course redesigns. He then looked ahead, founding National Innovation Day, leading Lifesaving CPR's rollout and chairing the Expanding Horizons Review.

**Version 2, story-led draft [393 / 400 words]**

> Chris Jacobson's story is one of dedication that has never paused, from a young lifesaver joining Ulverstone SLSC in 2004 to one of the most influential volunteer leaders in modern Australian lifesaving. He stepped onto the national stage at the 2006 SLSA Youth Development Forum at the start of his journey, and twenty years later he has never left it. By 2011/12 he was the SLSA Australian Surf Lifesaver of the Year, the youngest ever recipient and the only Tasmanian to receive the award. He went on to serve fourteen years on the Lifesaving Members Advisory Committee (2012-2025), and in 2016 became the youngest person to hold the role of National Chair of Lifesaving, serving nine years.
>
> As National Chair he transformed the nation's approach to lifesaving, lifting practices and standards across the movement and delivering that change through training and education. He chaired the National Lifesaving Curriculum Review, chaired the rewrite of the Public Safety and Aquatic Rescue manual and delivered its first online interactive edition, chaired the redesign of the Bronze Medallion, Silver Medallion Aquatic Rescue and Silver Medallion Patrol Captain courses, and built the first nationally delivered blended learning model for the Surf Rescue Certificate and Bronze Medallion, work recognised with an international award for best use of blended learning in 2020. These reforms continue to shape the development of thousands of new and existing members every year.
>
> He has always looked to the movement's future. He founded the inaugural National Innovation Day, conceived and chaired the Expanding Horizons Review, now a core strategic project confronting the movement's future volunteer and service delivery challenges, and served as National Implementation Lead for Lifesaving CPR (2024-2026), the first nationally coordinated CPR quality improvement initiative implemented by a community first aid organisation in Australia and internationally at scale. As Chair of Lifesaving he also introduced the Youth Surf Life Saver of the Year award, demonstrating his commitment to empowering our young members as the future of our organisation.
>
> Through all of it he has remained a frontline volunteer. He still patrols today at Elouera SLSC, remains a member and an asset to Ulverstone SLSC and Tasmania long after moving interstate, and gives so much of himself that members around the country routinely assume he is paid staff. He is not. He is a volunteer, and that is just one measure of his dedication.

